-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+On acceptance the server assigns a **revision_id** and records the author's identity and server receipt time alongside the payload. The response includes the revision_id and the visit's resulting state.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Visit States and Retrieval
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+Retrieval returns the visit's current state and the relevant revision(s):
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+- **current state**: the single head revision.
+- **contested state**: all competing head revisions.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Visits in contested state are excluded from the export-eligible set.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+A new submission accepted against a base that is no longer the current head — for example, one whose base was a revision that previously lost a resolution — is still accepted. The visit re-enters contested state if the submission creates a second head alongside the existing current head.
+
+## Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves a contested visit by designating one of its competing head revisions as the **accepted revision**. The server records: the coordinator's identity, the winning revision_id, the full set of competing revision_ids at the time of resolution, and the resolution timestamp. The designated revision becomes the sole head and the visit returns to current state.
+
+The resolution operation is valid only for visits in contested state and only when the named revision is among the current competing heads.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions: competing complete revisions held for coordinator review | Field-aware three-way merge: auto-combine disjoint field changes, review overlapping ones | Merge rules would need to understand species identification and cover estimate relationships. The team prefers a larger review queue over rules that could silently combine scientifically related fields without noticing. Original submissions are preserved under either policy. |
+| Base revision validation | Require base_revision_id to be a known revision for the same visit | Accept any UUID without server validation | Catching an unknown base at submission time prevents silent data loss from a tablet operating on a corrupted or stale revision ID. |
+| Resolution mechanism | Coordinator designates one competing revision as winner | Auto-select most recent; coordinator submits a new composite revision | Designating one revision is auditable and preserves the worker's submitted intent intact. The record-store design accommodates a composite if review-export later adds a coordinator submission flow — a coordinator-authored revision would be submitted like any other revision. |
+| Accept submissions from non-head bases | Accept; may re-contest the visit | Reject submissions whose base is not the current head | Rejecting would discard field work from a tablet that had not yet synced the resolution outcome. The "do not silently discard a submitted edit" tenet requires accepting the submission. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+1. Maximum number of competing revisions before the system surfaces a coordinator alert — review-export concern.
+2. Whether exports carry full revision history alongside the current head or only the current head — review-export concern.
+3. Whether coordinators may submit a composite revision as a resolution rather than choosing among competing revisions — review-export concern; the record-store submission model already supports this if the composite is submitted as a new revision citing one of the competing revisions as its base.
+4. Required vs. optional payload fields within a visit (e.g., whether notes are optional) — likely defined jointly with site-catalog.
+
